WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.657
Accepting a file is different from trusting it.

2
00:00:02.657 --> 00:00:08.966
Uploads cross a major trust boundary because the client supplies content,
metadata, and often a suggested name.

3
00:00:08.966 --> 00:00:19.061
A safe upload design defines size limits, file type expectations,
ownership, storage location, access control, retention, and downstream
processing before files are accepted.

4
00:00:19.061 --> 00:00:24.441
The upload endpoint should be treated as a controlled intake process, not
a casual write to disk.

5
00:00:24.441 --> 00:00:28.027
Content type and extension checks are not enough by themselves.

6
00:00:28.027 --> 00:00:33.872
A browser, client, or caller may provide misleading metadata, and file
contents may not match the label.

7
00:00:33.872 --> 00:00:42.307
The application should validate content for its intended use, choose
parsers carefully, and avoid assuming that a familiar extension proves
safety.

8
00:00:42.307 --> 00:00:47.288
Server-generated filenames reduce the risk created by user-controlled
storage names.

9
00:00:47.288 --> 00:00:53.465
Uploaded files should usually be stored outside executable paths and
outside application code directories.

10
00:00:53.465 --> 00:01:01.036
That reduces the chance that uploaded content is treated as code,
templates, public static content, or directly executable material.

11
00:01:01.036 --> 00:01:12.393
Quarantine concepts, malware scanning at a high level, and delayed
promotion workflows can be useful when files need additional inspection
before they become available to users or downstream systems.

12
00:01:12.393 --> 00:01:16.179
Download authorization is just as important as upload validation.

13
00:01:16.179 --> 00:01:20.629
A file existing on the server does not mean the requester is allowed to
access it.

14
00:01:20.629 --> 00:01:26.075
The application should check ownership, role, tenant, or business rule
before serving content.

15
00:01:26.075 --> 00:01:35.240
Retention and deletion rules should also be explicit so sensitive uploads
are not kept longer than needed or left behind after accounts, cases, or
workflows close.
